var e=1,t=`pr-liquidity-01`,n=`portfolio-risk`,r=`Liquidity, the Denominator Effect and Commitment Pacing`,i=`Public markets fall, private marks do not, and a portfolio finds itself over-allocated to the assets it cannot sell.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`pr-riskmeasures-01`,`funds-calls-01`],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`An allocation to private markets creates three obligations that a public portfolio does not have, and all three land at once in a bad market.

**Uncalled [[commitment|commitments]].** Capital promised and not yet drawn. A liability with an unknown schedule, and failing to meet a [[capital-call|call]] is a default with severe penalties.

**No exit.** The assets cannot be sold at their marks, or often at all, within any useful timeframe. A secondary sale is possible at a discount that widens exactly when you need it.

**Stale valuations.** The marks move quarterly and lag. Which produces the specific problem this topic is about.

**The [[denominator-effect|denominator effect]].** Public markets fall, private marks do not move, so the private allocation as a percentage of the total portfolio rises. Nothing was bought. The portfolio is now over-allocated to illiquid assets, potentially in breach of its own policy limits, and the only assets it can sell to rebalance are the ones that already fell.

**It is a measurement artefact with entirely real consequences**, and it has forced institutional selling in every major drawdown of the past twenty years.`},{type:`concept`,id:`the-arithmetic`,body:`**The denominator effect is arithmetic, so it can be anticipated exactly rather than discovered.**

Start with 1,000: 700 public, 300 private — a 30% private allocation against a 30% target.

Public markets fall 30%. Private marks are unchanged, because the quarter has not closed and the valuer sees no reason to move.

- Public: **490**
- Private: **300**
- Total: **790**
- Private allocation: **38.0%**

**An eight-point breach of a 30% target, with no transaction having taken place.**

To restore 30%, the portfolio would need to reduce private assets by **63** — in the exact market where secondary buyers are demanding steep discounts, and where the marks being used for the rebalancing calculation are themselves about to fall.

The compounding problem is that **uncalled commitments keep drawing**. The private allocation grows while the portfolio shrinks, because the commitments were made against a larger portfolio in a better market.

So three things happen simultaneously: the allocation breaches, the calls arrive, and the liquid assets available to meet them have just fallen in value. **That is not three problems — it is one, and it is foreseeable from the commitment schedule.**`},{type:`intuition`,id:`pacing`,body:`**Commitment pacing is the practical answer, and it is a cash-flow model rather than an allocation decision.**

The insight is that a private markets programme is a series of overlapping cash flow streams: commitments drawn over four or five years, distributions returned over ten, all running simultaneously across vintages. In a mature programme distributions from older funds substantially fund calls on newer ones — which is why the net cash requirement of a steady-state programme is far smaller than its headline commitments.

The implications are unintuitive and important:

**A programme being built is much more cash-hungry than one at steady state.** The first five years require real funding; afterwards it becomes closer to self-financing.

**Stopping commitments does not stop the calls.** Existing commitments continue drawing for years. A pause reduces future obligations and does nothing about current ones.

**Distributions slow exactly when calls accelerate.** In a downturn, exits stop and managers call capital to support portfolios or to buy cheaply. **The two streams that normally offset each other move the same way instead.**

**Which gives the test that matters: model calls and distributions under a stress scenario where distributions stop for two years and calls continue.** The resulting cash requirement is the liquidity that must genuinely be available — and it is usually much larger than the number in the policy document.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-denominator`,body:`A 1,000 portfolio: 700 public, 300 private, against a 30% private target. Public markets fall 30% and private marks are unchanged.`,walkthrough:[`Public falls to 700 × 0.70 = **490**. Private stays at **300**.`,`New total = **790**.`,`Private allocation = 300 / 790 = **38.0%**, against a 30% target.`,`**Read that carefully: an 8-point allocation breach caused entirely by the other assets falling.** No private asset changed hands or changed value.`,`To restore the target, private assets must fall to 0.30 × 790 = 237, so **63 would have to be sold** — roughly a fifth of the private book.`,`In that market, secondary buyers are pricing private assets at meaningful discounts to their marks. Selling 63 of marked value might realise 50.`,`And the marks themselves will fall next quarter, so the breach partially self-corrects — for anyone who did nothing.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **before setting a private markets target, compute the allocation after a 30% public drawdown with private marks unchanged.** If that figure breaches your policy limit, the limit will force a sale at the worst moment — so either the target or the limit needs changing now, while it is cheap.`]},{type:`table`,id:`liquidity-table`,caption:`What happens to each source of liquidity in a stress event`,headers:[`Source`,`In normal markets`,`In stress`],rows:[[`Distributions from mature funds`,`A reliable offset to new calls`,`Stop — exits close and managers hold assets.`],[`Public asset sales`,`Straightforward`,`Available, and you sell at depressed prices into the fall.`],[`Secondary sales of fund stakes`,`Modest discount`,`Steep discount, and the buyer knows you are forced.`],[`Credit facility`,`Cheap and available`,`Repriced or withdrawn, exactly when needed.`],[`Cash`,`Yields least — the reason it gets minimised`,`The only source that behaves as expected.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Policy limits that force selling.** A hard private markets cap becomes a forced-sale trigger in a drawdown. Ranges with explicit tolerance for the denominator effect are the standard fix, and they have to be written before the event.

**Modelling calls without a stress case.** Base-case pacing assumes distributions continue. The scenario that matters is the one where they stop for two years.

**Counting a credit facility as liquidity.** Facilities are repriced or pulled in stress. Committed, documented and multi-year is different from a relationship arrangement.

**Over-committing in good markets.** Commitments are sized against current portfolio value. When the portfolio falls, the commitments do not, and the ratio deteriorates.

**Treating the secondary market as an exit.** It exists and it clears at prices reflecting your urgency. A forced seller in a bad market is the worst-positioned participant.

**Ignoring [[subscription-line|subscription lines]] in pacing models.** Facilities delay calls, which makes the timing less predictable — the call arrives later and larger, and possibly in a worse market.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`liquidity-is-an-asset`,body:`**Cash is the only asset that behaves exactly as expected in a crisis, and it is the one every allocator is under pressure to minimise.**

The pressure is understandable. Cash yields least, drags on reported returns, and holding it looks like an absence of decision. Committees ask why it is there.

The answer is that liquidity is an option, and options have value that does not appear in a yield. Specifically it buys three things:

**The ability to meet calls without selling.** Which is the difference between a programme that survives a drawdown and one that is dismantled by it.

**The ability to buy.** Every dislocation transfers value from forced sellers to whoever has cash. That transfer is the largest single source of return available in a crisis, and it goes to the prepared rather than the clever.

**Freedom from the governance spiral.** A portfolio that can meet its obligations is not having emergency meetings, which is worth more than any spread.

**So the honest framing: a liquidity buffer is not idle capital, it is a paid-for option on the best opportunity set of the cycle.** The premium is the yield given up, and it is computable — a 5% buffer forgoing 3% of excess return costs 15bp a year of portfolio return.

**Fifteen [[basis-point|basis points]] a year to avoid being a forced seller is not an expensive option.** It is worth saying in those terms, because "why are we holding cash" is much harder to answer than "here is what the option costs and what it buys".`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Compute the private allocation after a 30% public drawdown with private marks held flat.** If it breaches the policy limit, fix the limit now rather than under duress.

**Model calls and distributions with distributions stopped for two years.** The resulting cash need is the real liquidity requirement.

**Express the liquidity buffer as an option premium in basis points.** It converts an uncomfortable conversation about idle cash into a priced decision.

**Check whether policy limits are hard caps or ranges.** A hard cap is a forced-sale mechanism dressed as prudence.

**Ask what committed liquidity facilities exist and on what terms.** Uncommitted lines are not liquidity — they are an expectation, and it is tested precisely when it fails.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`A 30% public drawdown with flat private marks takes a 30% private allocation to 38.0% — an eight-point breach with no transaction at all.`,`Restoring the target would require selling about a fifth of the private book into a market where buyers are pricing discounts to marks.`,`Distributions and calls normally offset; in stress both move the same way — exits close and calls continue. Model that scenario, not the base case.`,`Cash is a priced option: a 5% buffer forgoing 3% of excess return costs 15bp a year, and it buys the ability to meet calls and to buy from forced sellers.`]}],u=[{type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`liquidity`,`definition`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`What is the denominator effect?`,choices:[`Private asset valuations falling faster than public ones`,`Public markets falling while private marks stay flat, so the private allocation rises as a share of a smaller total`,`Capital calls arriving faster than expected`,`A reduction in the number of available managers`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The effect arises precisely because private marks do not fall as fast.`,`Correct. Nothing is bought or sold — the ratio moves because the denominator shrank, which can breach a policy limit and force a sale.`,`Calls compound the problem and are a separate mechanism.`,`Unrelated to valuation arithmetic.`],explanation:`Naming it as a denominator problem rather than a valuation problem points at the fix: allocation ranges rather than hard caps, so a mechanical breach does not require a sale into the worst available market.`,id:`pr-liquidity-01-q1`},{id:`pr-liquidity-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`liquidity`,`denominator`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-denominator`,stem:`A portfolio holds 700 public and 300 private. Public assets fall 30% and private marks are unchanged. What is the private allocation now, in percent? One decimal place.`,answer:38,tolerance:.4,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`%`,explanation:`An eight-point breach with no transaction. This is the calculation to run before setting any private markets target, because it determines whether the policy limit will force a sale in a drawdown.`},{id:`pr-liquidity-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:4,tags:[`liquidity`,`rebalancing`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-denominator`,stem:`Same portfolio after the fall: 490 public, 300 private, total 790. How much private value must be sold to restore a 30% target? One decimal place.`,answer:63,tolerance:2,toleranceType:`abs`,explanation:`About a fifth of the private book, into a secondary market pricing discounts to marks. And the marks will fall next quarter anyway, so the breach partly self-corrects for anyone who did nothing — which is the argument for ranges rather than hard caps.`},{id:`pr-liquidity-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`liquidity`,`denominator`],concept:`the-arithmetic`,stem:`Why is the denominator effect dangerous even though it involves no change in the private assets?`,choices:[`Because it reduces the value of the private assets`,`Because it can breach policy limits and force sales of illiquid assets in exactly the market where they are hardest to sell`,`Because it triggers additional capital calls`,`Because it changes the private assets' valuation methodology`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The private assets are unchanged in value — that is precisely what causes the effect.`,`Correct. The breach is mechanical and the consequence is a governance-driven sale at the worst possible time, in the least liquid part of the portfolio.`,`Calls follow the fund's deployment schedule and are not triggered by the allocation ratio, though they do compound the problem.`,`The methodology is unaffected; only the ratio moves.`],explanation:`The fix is written in advance: allocation ranges rather than hard caps, with explicit language that the denominator effect does not require immediate rebalancing. It costs nothing to include and it prevents the most predictable forced sale in institutional investing.`},{id:`pr-liquidity-01-q5`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`liquidity`,`misconception`],concept:`pacing`,stem:`"We can pause commitments if liquidity gets tight." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — existing commitments keep drawing for years, so a pause reduces future obligations and does nothing about the calls already promised`,`False — commitments can be cancelled at any time without penalty`,`True — pausing new commitments solves a liquidity problem`,`False — because managers never call capital in a downturn`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. A pause is a decision about the next vintage. The current uncalled balance is a contractual liability that continues to draw, often faster in a downturn.`,`Failing to fund a call is a default with severe penalties, typically forfeiting a large share of the interest.`,`It helps in future years and not in the year the liquidity is short.`,`They frequently call more in a downturn — to support portfolio companies and to buy cheaply.`],explanation:`The practical consequence is that liquidity for private markets has to be planned years ahead, because the tool available in the moment — pausing — has almost no near-term effect. That is what makes the stress-case pacing model the actual control.`},{id:`pr-liquidity-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`liquidity`,`stress`,`on-the-job`],concept:`pacing`,stem:`In a stress scenario, what happens to distributions and capital calls?`,choices:[`Both slow, so the net effect is neutral`,`Distributions stop as exits close, while calls continue or accelerate — the two streams that normally offset each other move the same way`,`Distributions accelerate as managers realise assets`,`Calls stop because managers cannot find deals`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Calls do not slow proportionally; managers often draw more to support portfolios or deploy into dislocation.`,`Correct. In normal conditions distributions substantially fund calls. In stress that offset disappears exactly when the liquid portfolio has also fallen.`,`Exits become harder and slower, so distributions fall rather than rise.`,`Dislocations are when managers most want capital to deploy.`],explanation:`This correlation is the entire reason a base-case pacing model is inadequate. The scenario to size liquidity against is distributions at zero for two years with calls continuing — and that number is materially larger than the base case suggests.`},{id:`pr-liquidity-01-q7`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`liquidity`,`policy`,`on-the-job`],concept:`liquidity-is-an-asset`,stem:`How is a liquidity buffer best presented to an investment committee?`,choices:[`As a prudent reserve required by policy`,`As a priced option: a 5% buffer forgoing 3% of excess return costs 15bp a year, and it buys the ability to meet calls and to buy from forced sellers`,`As cash awaiting deployment`,`As a hedge against equity risk`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`"Prudence" is unarguable and unquantified, which makes it easy to erode when returns are being compared.`,`Correct. Pricing it makes the trade-off explicit and defensible, and the number is small relative to what it prevents.`,`Describing it as awaiting deployment concedes that holding it is a failure to act.`,`It behaves defensively and is not a hedge in any specific sense.`],explanation:`Framing it as a premium changes the discussion from "why are we holding cash" — which has no good answer — to "is 15bp a year a fair price for not being a forced seller", which usually does.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
